      "dom_id": "question-4101",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find $x+y$ if the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(x,y)$ and $(5,-4)$ is $(0,6).$",
      "worked_solution": "The given points are ${{(x,y)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$, ${{(5,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(0,6)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}.$\n\nFirst, the $x$-coordinate of the midpoint's  must be the average of the $x$-coordinates of the endpoints: \n\n$$\n0=\\frac{x+5}{2}\\,⇒\\,x=-5.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the $y$-coordinate of the midpoint must be the average of the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints: \n\n$$\n6=\\frac{y+(-4)}{2}\\,⇒\\,y=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(x,y)=(-5,16)$ and $x+y=11.$",
